#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120913} :activity/id #uuid "7247ca88-471f-5303-aa77-7e868bf58346" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048377 :topic/id #uuid "9a5bcec0-860b-5cc9-83b6-331b8b7e1d13" :topic/math-academy-id 1792} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120913 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121135 :tutorial/content "Consider the curve $C$ parametrized by the vector-valued function \n\n$$\n\\mathbf f(t) = \\langle t+1, \\: t^2 +1\\rangle, \\qquad t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this function, we get\n\n$$\n\\mathbf f'(t) = \\langle 1, \\: 2t\\rangle.\n$$\n\nEvaluating this function and its derivative at $t=0,$ we get the following pair of vectors:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf f(0)  =  \\left\\langle 1, 1 \\right\\rangle, \\qquad \\mathbf f'(0)  =  \\left\\langle 1, 0 \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nIf we view $\\mathbf{f}'(0)$ as a vector attached to the tip of the position vector $\\mathbf f(0),$ then it will be tangent to the curve $C,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/85/854d16c788836f784d739f73063354fa3ca2ad233f03cce53b99e5764449f585.png)\n\nSince $\\mathbf f'(0) \\neq \\mathbf{0},$ it serves as a direction vector for the tangent line to the curve at $\\mathbf f(0).$ The equation of this line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(u) & =f(0)+u{f}^{'}(0) \\\\  & =⟨1,1⟩+u⟨1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1+u,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $u \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\nIn general, if a curve $C$ is parametrized by a vector function $\\mathbf f(t),$ then the vector $\\mathbf f'(t)$ gives a **tangent vector** to $C$ at $\\mathbf{f} (t),$ provided that $\\mathbf f'(t)\\neq \\mathbf{0}.$\n\nFor a fixed $t$ in the domain of $\\mathbf{f}(t),$ the equation of the corresponding tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{R}(u) = \\mathbf{f}(t) + u\\mathbf{f}'(t), \\qquad u \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).\n$$\n\nFurthermore, the vector $\\mathbf f'(t)$ always points in the direction that the curve $C$ is oriented. In our example above, as $t$ increases and passes through the point $(1,1),$ the curve is transversed from left to right, and the direction of the vector $\\mathbf{f}'(0)$ is also from left to right." :tutorial/id #uuid "dbd32d96-f4fc-5f66-861c-fc1b9746be10" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7864 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "2d2f798c-9ffb-57a0-aea4-4212795c1dcc" :step/math-academy-id 45344 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120914}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120914 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119943 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119513 :question/id #uuid "7d93dd10-5319-58e7-9106-f8c675dc9e65" :question/math-academy-id "e-12567"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9c2fbd5-33cf-51fb-b95d-cf5277f3e4ae" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048999}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120212} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120214} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262642} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262643} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262644} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262645} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345043} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345044} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345045}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Vector"} :step/id #uuid "989791d3-66ad-598a-a711-f5c1c0da6f59" :step/math-academy-id 45345 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120915}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120915 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119944 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119514 :question/id #uuid "42109de0-eb59-53f9-982e-1433f630b067" :question/math-academy-id "e-12568"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "60e28b26-0eb7-51ed-bcf9-2660b98a6028" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049485}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120108} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120215} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262656} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262657} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304794} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308635} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308643} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308650} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308704} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345046} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363010} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400240} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405125}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Line to a Curve"} :step/id #uuid "e0c03cce-2d21-571e-98fe-f72a42288e4a" :step/math-academy-id 45348 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120917}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120916 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119945 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119515 :question/id #uuid "26c3579b-5b24-5e84-9024-5b2597856d20" :question/math-academy-id "e-12569"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4636870-89db-5bf9-8a04-777407957e33" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049715}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120109} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120216} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262664} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284532} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284533} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284534} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284535} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284536} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308725} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311545} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363527} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398604} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410824}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Tangent Vector is Parallel to Another Vector"} :step/id #uuid "74ac7b94-509d-54d2-9176-80fb0b50e930" :step/math-academy-id 45351} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120917 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119946 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119516 :question/id #uuid "e8c24f26-7658-5251-922a-4b34043a71d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-12580"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "297a4812-a87a-592a-818a-8c8d3d3f7868" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048954}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120253} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120522} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262662} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262663} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284525} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284526} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284527} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284528} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284529} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345047} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345102} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345103}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Tangent Vector is Perpendicular to Another Vector"} :step/id #uuid "ad5a98da-8ea4-5205-9db2-94f0f8b668ab" :step/math-academy-id 45392 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120916}}] :activity/title "Tangent Vectors and Tangent Lines to Curves" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120011}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410826} :activity/id #uuid "27a4662b-38f4-5da4-a14f-0ae0dbfbda48" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048377 :topic/id #uuid "9a5bcec0-860b-5cc9-83b6-331b8b7e1d13" :topic/math-academy-id 1792} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410825 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410824 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38f7dbd3-9ac6-59ba-822d-baee2e1a48d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(3,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410818} {:answer/id #uuid "4ad7606a-78cf-5b5a-aad3-516d0783136b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410819} {:answer/id #uuid "6e2a15f9-1731-51c6-a7aa-e028d3d2c84e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(0,7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410820} {:answer/id #uuid "b2e66482-ad5e-56f0-ab9c-0187ad900545" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(0,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410821} {:answer/id #uuid "c15d36f2-d954-5b67-a997-5ab2ddb7afaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(8,-9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410822}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410818} :answer-field/id #uuid "236f2410-bb87-524a-b10a-c030da587949" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410823}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "eb19afc5-d677-5f22-bac0-92428e650b61" :question/math-academy-id "q-126900" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2}-2t,4t-1⟩,$ find the point at which the tangent to the curve is parallel to the vector $⟨-1,1⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t-2,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $⟨-1\\,,\\,1⟩$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})=c⟨-1,1⟩.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =c⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\ ⟨2t-2,4⟩ & =c⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\ ⟨2t-2,4⟩ & =⟨-c,c⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2t-2=-c \\\\ 4=c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we obtain that $c=4.$ Substituting $c=4$ into the first equation, we get $t=-1.$ \n\nTherefore, $⟨-1,1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=-1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-1) & =⟨(-1)^{2}-2(-1),4(-1)-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,-5⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨-1,1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $(3,-5).$"} :step/id #uuid "25cc0c4b-5364-577c-92a1-008375ee5f34" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410828}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410826 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345046 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bdb4010-1e08-5378-a110-856c1b0d31e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩+u⟨1,\\,1,\\,-1⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342732} {:answer/id #uuid "8844755e-17ca-5997-8087-0445418bac62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩+u⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343418} {:answer/id #uuid "97e8fdd1-1609-5108-82df-e53e59510619" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,1,1⟩+u⟨1,\\,0,\\,1⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343570} {:answer/id #uuid "d3b6012c-303d-5b23-b10c-010c9c93ca49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,1,1⟩+u⟨1,\\,-1,0⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344097} {:answer/id #uuid "fd335b51-418d-5e75-99ad-992ab400d4c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩+u⟨0,\\,-1,\\,1⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344464}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342732} :answer-field/id #uuid "c171024e-4b6d-5601-9cbd-861fac1471b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344833}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a928b0be-2c4b-5a25-9350-6790b8cc0365" :question/math-academy-id "q-75203" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve $r(t)=⟨t,\\,{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{-t}⟩$ at the point where $t=0,$  valid for all  real values of $u?$" :question/worked-solution "At the point where $t=0$,  the vector ${r}^{′}(0)$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n\n \n\n$$\nR(u)=r(0)+u{r}^{′}(0),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find $r(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,\\,{e}^{0},\\,{e}^{-0}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,{e}^{t},\\,-{e}^{-t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(0) & =⟨1,\\,{e}^{0},\\,-{e}^{-0}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point \n where $t=0$ is\n\n$$\nR(u)=⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩+u⟨1,\\,1,\\,-1⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "588c5c9a-8d3d-51b8-8b90-dff365fed144" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410825}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410827 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284525 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a91054f-adf7-5f56-83c2-fa3119dc478c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{16},-\\frac{7}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282606} {:answer/id #uuid "88ea9708-0042-5422-b8c9-124b38c78848" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(64,2)$ and $(\\frac{1}{16},-\\frac{7}{4})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282871} {:answer/id #uuid "cbc619de-3849-50d7-a032-ed5d0fa3212b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(64,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283469} {:answer/id #uuid "dc37b06f-ca47-55ac-9f76-e24057ce58fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2\\,,\\,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283609} {:answer/id #uuid "f38a4e4e-a50c-5a8c-9c70-c08d603abc61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(2,-1)$ and $(\\frac{1}{16},-\\frac{7}{4})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283818}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283609} :answer-field/id #uuid "109ae18e-4733-5298-895a-59e0b555d56b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283966}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "854e79dd-e49f-5d57-896b-a1669e5ffce7" :question/math-academy-id "q-164833" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨2{t}^{5}\\,,\\,{t}^{2}-2⟩,$ $t\\in (0,\\infty ),$ find all of the points at which the tangent to the curve is perpendicular to the vector $⟨1\\,,\\,-5⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{5})\\,,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨10{t}^{4}\\,,\\,2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors  $⟨1\\,,\\,-5⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $⟨1\\,,\\,-5⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨1\\,,\\,-5⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨1\\,,\\,-5⟩\\cdot ⟨10{t}^{4}\\,,\\,2t⟩ & =0 \\\\ 1\\cdot 10{t}^{4}+(-5)\\cdot 2t & =0 \\\\ 10{t}^{4}-10t & =0 \\\\ 10t({t}^{3}-1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\in (0,\\infty )$, the only solution is $t=1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨2(1)^{5}\\,,\\,(1)^{2}-2⟩=⟨2\\,,\\,-1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n we conclude that $⟨1\\,,\\,-5⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(2\\,,\\,-1).$"} :step/id #uuid "677b5852-f95c-533d-b31c-e87dce9ad2d7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410829}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410828 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120109 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8311c425-96db-50b4-bda2-7afc21298e5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(0,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261013} {:answer/id #uuid "970d51ce-6cc7-567e-b921-0a5f575739b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(10,-7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261184} {:answer/id #uuid "aed5ed6e-37c8-5e3c-ae55-c95e30ce3202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(6,-7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261416} {:answer/id #uuid "bc52c38c-023f-560d-827e-ce988bae6a2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261533} {:answer/id #uuid "c912ae60-77c2-546c-be59-6f64006178e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(3,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261652}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261533} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c61931c-b6de-57e6-b862-b7df723296cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262146}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "22d6e6f4-daf0-5645-a375-f0c335545e70" :question/math-academy-id "q-126902" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨2{t}^{2}-t,5-3{t}^{2}⟩,$ find the point at which the tangent to the curve is parallel to the vector $⟨-1,2⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}-t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5-3{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4t-1,-6t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $⟨-1\\,,\\,2⟩$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})=c⟨-1,2⟩.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =c⟨-1,2⟩ \\\\ ⟨4t-1,-6t⟩ & =c⟨-1,2⟩ \\\\ ⟨4t-1,-6t⟩ & =⟨-c,2c⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4t-1=-c \\\\ -6t=2c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain that $c=-4t+1.$ Substituting $c=-4t+1$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6t & =2(-4t+1) \\\\ -6t & =-8t+2 \\\\ 2t & =2 \\\\ t & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $⟨-1,2⟩$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨2(1)^{2}-1,5-3(1)^{2}⟩=⟨1,2⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨-1,2⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $(1,2).$"} :step/id #uuid "881f99cb-89c3-507b-a7a0-8ef265378f7b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410827}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410829 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284532 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "580c3843-6361-5a93-8e93-aa4eecc3f021" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(24,26)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282438} {:answer/id #uuid "95ec2ae6-b848-5b2c-9e73-71480e6476c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-9,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282983} {:answer/id #uuid "9978da76-99f6-51aa-835d-d6470ba9b397" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-7,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283023} {:answer/id #uuid "c39a49da-d2c5-5e18-8270-20d823e49792" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-9,-4)$ and $(-8,-6)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283395} {:answer/id #uuid "ca04d778-71e9-52fd-ba77-a58b5aaf9a03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-7,-4)$ and $(24,26)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283453}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282438} :answer-field/id #uuid "543918ac-60ea-556f-87e9-9fe723e51c77" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284072}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5a127c57-e392-5180-8b85-86041bead0c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-165038" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{5}-8,2{t}^{4}-6⟩,$ $t\\in (0,\\infty )$, find the point at which the tangent to the curve is parallel to the vector $⟨1,\\frac{4}{5}⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{5}-8),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{4}-6)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5{t}^{4},8{t}^{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $⟨1,\\frac{4}{5}⟩$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})=c⟨1,\\frac{4}{5}⟩.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =c⟨1,\\frac{4}{5}⟩ \\\\ ⟨5{t}^{4},8{t}^{3}⟩ & =c⟨1,\\frac{4}{5}⟩ \\\\ ⟨5{t}^{4},8{t}^{3}⟩ & =⟨c,\\frac{4c}{5}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5{t}^{4}=c \\\\ 8{t}^{3}=\\frac{4c}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain that $c=5{t}^{4}.$ Substituting $c=5{t}^{4}$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{t}^{3} & =\\frac{4(5{t}^{4})}{5} \\\\ 8{t}^{3}-4{t}^{4} & =0 \\\\ 4{t}^{3}(2-t) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\in (0,\\infty )$, the only solution is $t=2.$ \n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(2) & =⟨(2)^{5}-8,2(2)^{4}-6⟩=⟨24,26⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨1,\\frac{4}{5}⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $(24,26).$"} :step/id #uuid "c80e8392-766a-5d4f-9dc1-c5ef5cb2463f"}] :activity/title "Tangent Vectors and Tangent Lines to Curves" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410817}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055877} :activity/id #uuid "16bd6886-0670-51ea-9c62-c70c7e015ce4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050088 :topic/id #uuid "2c984580-e283-5888-86e7-f792567f1a8a" :topic/math-academy-id 55} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055877 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056003 :tutorial/content "Interesting mathematical problems often involve a large (or infinite) collection of sets. Labeling each set with a subscript is often convenient in such situations. \n\nFor example, suppose we define a collection of sets $A_i$ for $i\\in\\{1,2,3,\\ldots\\} = \\mathbb N$ as follows:\n\n$$\nA_1 = \\{1,2\\}, \\qquad A_2 = \\{3,4\\}, \\qquad A_3 = \\{5,6\\}, \\qquad \\ldots\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- This collection of $A_i$'s, which we denote $\\big\\{ A_i \\big\\}_{i \\in \\mathbb N},$ is called a **family of indexed sets.**\n\n- The set $i=\\{1,2,3,\\ldots\\} = \\mathbb N$ is called the **index set**.\n\nWriting each set with a subscript makes talking about a general $A_i$ easier. In this particular case, for example, we can express a general member of this family in terms of its index:\n\n$$\nA_i = \\{2i-1,\\, 2i\\}, \\qquad i\\in\\mathbb N\n$$\n\nSo, if we wanted to write down the elements of $A_{100},$ we substitute $i=100$ into the definition of $A_i{:}$\n\n$$\nA_{100} = \\big\\{2(100)-1,\\, 2(100)\\big\\} = \\{199,200\\}\n$$\n\nWe can use any index set when defining a family of indexed sets. \n\nFor example, consider the indexed family $\\big\\{ B_x \\big\\}_{x \\in \\mathbb R},$ where $B_x$ is the real interval defined by\n\n$$\nB_x = \\big[ 0, x^2 \\big), \\qquad x \\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nHere, the index set is $\\mathbb{R}.$ \n\nTo write down, say, the elements of $B_{\\color{blue}\\sqrt{5}},$ we substitute $x={\\color{blue}\\sqrt{5}}$ into our definition of $B_x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}_{\\sqrt{5}} & =[0,\\,(\\sqrt{5})^{2}) \\\\  & =[0,5)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "94ce30ce-17fc-5813-9947-89a8d392343b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7957 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9a6cdf07-394b-572b-b1c9-fc2d60400ed0" :step/math-academy-id 70625 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055878}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055878 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054831 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054362 :question/id #uuid "154d4cf7-a72a-51b1-bbb3-9bcf83b88e98" :question/math-academy-id "e-12665"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f986c29-111e-5cb3-8104-62ab734f1c47" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055046} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055047} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055048} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284702} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284703} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284704} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284705} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284706}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements of an Indexed Family"} :step/id #uuid "0b2b9b72-b07b-51b4-970f-a98643e205c5" :step/math-academy-id 70626 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055879}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055879 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056033 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have the indexed family $\\{A_i\\}_{i\\in I}$ where $I$ is an index set. \n\nThe **union** of a family of indexed sets is the set containing all elements belonging to *at least one* set in the indexed family:\n\n$$\n\\bigcup_{i \\in I} A_i = \\big\\{ x\\in U \\: : \\: x \\in A_i \\:\\:\\textrm{for at least one } A_i,\\:\\: i \\in I \\big\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $U$ is a universal set.\n\nFor example, suppose we have the following family of indexed sets:\n\n$$\nX_i = \\{ -i, \\,0, \\, i \\}, \\qquad i\\in \\mathbb N\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the following union:\n\n$$\n\\bigcup_{i \\in \\mathbb N} X_i\n$$\n\nTo calculate our union, let's write down a few of our indexed sets:\n\n- If $i=1,$ we have $X_1 = \\{ -1, 0, 1 \\}.$\n\n- If $i=2,$ we have $X_2 = \\{ -2, 0, 2 \\}.$\n\n- If $i=3,$ we have $X_3 = \\{ -3, 0, 3 \\}.$\n\n- $\\cdots$\n\nand so on. \n\nNow, note the following:\n\n- It's clear that the $X_i$'s contain only integers. Therefore, any union of $X_i$'s will contain only integers. So, \n\n$$\n\\bigcup_{i \\in \\mathbb N} X_i  \\subseteq \\mathbb Z.\n$$\n\n- Notice that *any* integer $n$ belongs to our union. For instance, $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$ lies in $X_n=\\{ -n, 0, n \\}$ and, consequently, lies in the union of all $X_i$'s. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\bigcup_{i \\in \\mathbb N} X_i  \\supseteq \\mathbb Z.\n$$\n\nThus, using the fact that $A\\subseteq B$ and $B\\subseteq A$ if and only if $A=B,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{⋃}_{i\\in N}{X}_{i} & =\\overset{{X}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{{-1,0,1}}}\\cup \\overset{{X}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{{-2,0,2}}}\\cup \\overset{{X}_{3}}{\\overset{⏞}{{-3,0,3}}}\\cup ⋯=Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since our index set $I = \\mathbb N$ in this example, we can also use the following notation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{⋃}_{i=1}^{\\infty }{X}_{i}=Z\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "44240f43-3346-5c5f-9955-317e4e8ad24c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8177 :tutorial/title "Unions of Indexed  Families"} :step/id #uuid "2ec1fad8-799b-53d9-878f-444b40b1e568" :step/math-academy-id 70630 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055880}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055880 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056034 :tutorial/content "Similarly, the **intersection** of a family of indexed sets $\\{A_i\\}_{i\\in I}$ contains all elements that belong to *all* sets in our family:\n\n$$\n\\bigcap_{i \\in I} A_i = \\big\\{ x\\in U \\: : \\: x \\in A_i \\:\\:\\textrm{for all } A_i,\\:\\: i \\in I \\big\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $U$ is a universal set.\n\nFor example, suppose we have the following family of indexed sets:\n\n$$\nS_i = \\left[ 0, \\: 10^{-i} \\right], \\qquad i \\in \\mathbb{N}\n$$\n\nWe wish to compute the following intersection:\n\n$$\n\\bigcap_{i \\in \\mathbb{N}} S_i\n$$\n\nTo calculate our intersection, let's write down a few of our indexed sets:\n\n- If $i=1,$ we have $S_1 = [0, 0.1]$\n\n- If $i=2,$ we have $S_2 = \\left[ 0, 0.01 \\right]$\n\n- If $i=3,$ we have $S_3 = \\left[ 0, 0.001 \\right]$\n\n- $\\cdots$\n\nand so on.\n\nNow, note the following:\n\n- As $i$ increases, the right endpoint of $S_i$ approaches $0$ but never reaches it.\n\n- The left endpoint equals zero for each $S_i.$\n\nSo, the only element that lies in all sets is $0.$ Therefore, the intersection of our indexed family is the set containing zero only:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{⋂}_{i\\in N}{S}_{i}={0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since our index set $I = \\mathbb N$ in this example, we can also use the following notation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{⋂}_{i=1}^{\\infty }{S}_{i}={0}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "03cd6876-c452-576d-8f1a-24bb0fe077ea" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8178 :tutorial/title "Intersections of Indexed  Families"} :step/id #uuid "253c7481-555b-5d32-a3f7-49642ec94a10" :step/math-academy-id 70631 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055881}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055881 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054832 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054369 :question/id #uuid "60e24dac-c47a-53ee-9c91-0e6987e966f0" :question/math-academy-id "e-12763"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "957a2f61-61b1-5675-ba84-5348a47af986" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048899} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051033}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055049} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055050} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055092} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284710} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284711} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284712} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284713} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284714} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284715} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284716} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287880} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287881}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Unions and Intersections of Finite Families of Indexed Sets"} :step/id #uuid "c74f24a2-dcab-503c-9e0c-8b67b381e9c4" :step/math-academy-id 70632 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055882}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055882 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054833 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054370 :question/id #uuid "842ee2c1-8e78-56dd-b109-b9fcc273704e" :question/math-academy-id "e-12764"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c1e93acb-6c0b-5263-9df9-9dcc00680845" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055051} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055052} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055093} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284718} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284719} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284720} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284721} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287878} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287879} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287882} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287883} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363555}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Unions and Intersections of (Countably) Infinite Families of Indexed Sets"} :step/id #uuid "36a1848e-f2f5-52f9-9aad-05995f22fb49" :step/math-academy-id 70636}] :activity/title "Indexed Sets" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054911}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230052} :activity/id #uuid "50e3d3cb-c536-5096-8b7d-f9b0d3a94b49" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050392 :topic/id #uuid "45561e84-38ff-5ce1-9f3c-76fdda09730b" :topic/math-academy-id 6680} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230052 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230176 :tutorial/content "Consider the first-order inhomogeneous ODE with **constant coefficients**\n\n$$\na y' + b y = f(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants ($a \\neq 0$).\n\nAs we know, the method of finding the general solution is to find the sum of the complementary solution $y_c(x)$ and a particular solution $y_p(x).$\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x) + y_p(x)\n$$\n\nTo find the particular solution $y_p(x),$ we examine the functional form of the forcing term $f(x)$ and guess a form for $y_p(x)$ that matches it. \n\nAn ODE exhibits **sinusoidal forcing** if the forcing term $f(x)$ is a sine or cosine function, or a combination of both. It is typically written as\n\n$$\nf(x) = C \\cos(\\omega x) + D \\sin(\\omega x)\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ and $D$ are constants (not both zero).\n\nNow, if we choose only a sine or only a cosine as a trial function for the particular solution, differentiation will introduce the other sinusoidal term, since the derivative of a sinusoid is another sinusoid. Substituting such a trial solution into the ODE therefore produces both sine and cosine terms.\n\nTo ensure that this combination can match the forcing term $f(x),$ the trial guess *must* include both sine and cosine terms of the same frequency $\\omega,$ even when $f(x)$ itself contains only one of them.\n\nTherefore, our trial guess for the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\alpha \\cos(\\omega x) + \\beta \\sin(\\omega x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are to be determined.\n\nIn this lesson, we focus on solving first-order inhomogeneous ODEs with sinusoidal forcing. Let's look at a concrete example." :tutorial/id #uuid "16ccaf79-f39a-5c97-8b7e-a768583a8704" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14448 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b08bbcd6-b2d0-5926-b5e9-48a853cbad03" :step/math-academy-id 597581 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230053}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230053 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230230 :tutorial/content "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac {\\textrm {d} y} {\\textrm {d} x} + 2 y = 10\\sin x.\n$$\n\nThe associated homogeneous equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac {\\textrm {d} y} {\\textrm {d} x} + 2 y = 0\n$$\n\nand therefore, the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c(x) = Ae^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant. Let's find the particular solution $y_p(x)$ to this equation.\n\nSince the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is the sinusoidal function $f(x) = 10\\sin x,$ we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\alpha \\cos x + \\beta  \\sin x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} =   - \\alpha \\sin x +  \\beta \\cos x.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute $y_p$ and its derivative into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n( - \\alpha \\sin x +  \\beta \\cos x) + 2(\\alpha \\cos x + \\beta  \\sin x) = 10\\sin x.\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(2\\alpha+\\beta)\\cos x + (-\\alpha + 2\\beta) \\sin x = 10\\sin x + 0\\cos x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x$ on both sides of the equation, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha +\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}x) \\\\ -\\alpha +2\\beta =10\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha=-2, \\beta=4.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = -2\\cos x + 4\\sin x\n$$\n\nand the general solution to the equation is\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x) + y_p(x) = Ae^{-2x} -2\\cos x + 4\\sin x.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e265842b-cb73-5fe7-9aa0-d7cdf4b3625c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14704 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "0b2e8dc8-73e2-5e78-bf03-55587295628f" :step/math-academy-id 597582 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230054}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230054 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229166 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228899 :question/id #uuid "90e0e1d2-ca8a-5919-9891-599af549f2d5" :question/math-academy-id "e-21618"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6af40e66-155c-57b8-b427-95259a2c1034" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050789}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229629} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229640} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305073} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305091} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305092} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305113} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305134} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305136} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305137} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372638}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Sinusoidal Forcing"} :step/id #uuid "2e90e3f1-e6d3-565d-9cf8-a3fff2ab9c62" :step/math-academy-id 597583 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230055}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230055 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229167 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228900 :question/id #uuid "5769fd27-5fa4-5c85-b927-c87bedca3ea3" :question/math-academy-id "e-21619"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef7a2bbc-4172-591e-98eb-f7586d3ae06b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229630} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229648} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305115} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305116} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305127} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305128} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305129} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305131} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305138} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305139} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305140} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372628} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372633} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377132}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Sinusoidal Forcing"} :step/id #uuid "7f4df209-44ae-5584-b4b1-bb5e57ba48b3" :step/math-academy-id 597587 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230056}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230056 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229168 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228901 :question/id #uuid "3b584b5f-8398-5f18-923d-47a3fb09adca" :question/math-academy-id "e-21620"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "05d3144f-53da-5608-a07b-d1d5d672bf0b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229631} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229651} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308349} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308351} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308353} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308355} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308356} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308363} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308450} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308451}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"} :step/id #uuid "daae17f0-2223-5441-8a1a-41931c66d349" :step/math-academy-id 597591}] :activity/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs With Sinusoidal Forcing" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186229251}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120877} :activity/id #uuid "ea872253-9665-5737-9ff4-e1c4c5546bda" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050426 :topic/id #uuid "47f4b767-dd8b-5a07-a614-8ff85185d0eb" :topic/math-academy-id 1861} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120877 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121122 :tutorial/content "We say that two vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are **linearly independent** if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis $x_1 = x_2 = 0.$ Otherwise, if there exists any solution other than $x_1 = x_2 = 0,$ then we say that the two vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are **linearly dependent**.\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider two sets of vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/46/46c22d599752ded730a67bfd57b4dfe238e512f6dff2d561afaf2f145c8118c2.svg)\n\n- For the first set ${\\color{blue}(\\textrm{I})},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is true only if $x_1=x_2=0.$ So, the set $\\color{blue}(\\textrm{I})$ is *linearly independent*.\n\n- On the other hand, for the second set ${\\color{red}(\\textrm{II})},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has solutions other than $x_1=x_2=0.$ For example, $x_1=2$ and $x_2=1$ gives\n![](images/14/145f500e491087587478f03bd52ae180a7b4237acf17db0317ca07d28f389c56.svg)\nTherefore, the set $\\color{red}(\\textrm{II})$ is *not* linearly independent. Rather, it is *linearly dependent*.\n\n**Note:** You may notice that the vectors in the set $\\color{red}(\\textrm{II})$ were parallel (i.e. multiples of one another), while the vectors in set $\\color{blue}(\\textrm{I})$ were not. This trend holds in general, for all sets of two vectors:\n\n- a set of two vectors is *linearly dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel, while\n\n- a set of two vectors is *linearly independent* if and only if the two vectors are not parallel." :tutorial/id #uuid "938aba59-31f8-5ad9-8d3d-ce9287feb3de" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6811 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "8b84b116-3328-5055-9d5b-02360cff09e6" :step/math-academy-id 41352 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120878}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120878 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121123 :tutorial/content "Any set of two vectors that contains the zero vector $\\mathbf{0}$ must be linearly dependent. To see this, consider the set $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{0} \\right\\}.$ We can realize that this set is linearly dependent using either of the following two methods.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v} + 1 \\cdot \\mathbf{0} = \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v} + x_2 \\cdot \\mathbf{0} = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nhas a solution $x_1=0$ and $x_2=1,$ which is different from $x_1=x_2=0.$ So, the set is linearly dependent.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nAlternatively, recall that a set of two vectors is linearly dependent if and only if the two vectors are parallel. That is, the two vectors are multiples of each other.\n\nThe zero vector $\\mathbf{0}$ is parallel to every other vector because it is a multiple of any other vector. For any vector $\\mathbf{v},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{0} = 0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v} \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad \\mathbf{0} \\parallel \\mathbf{v}.\n$$\n\nSo, because $\\mathbf{0}$ is parallel to $\\mathbf{v},$ the set $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{0} \\right\\}$ is linearly dependent." :tutorial/id #uuid "ef2c96dd-d1cd-593e-a998-ba1be6c42686" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6812 :tutorial/title "Sets of Two Vectors Containing the Zero Vector"} :step/id #uuid "e814b4bf-688c-57df-8849-f4f2455723dd" :step/math-academy-id 41353 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120879}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120879 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119923 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119497 :question/id #uuid "cbabcd64-6a33-552d-9f2f-704a9df44d1e" :question/math-academy-id "e-10906"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5130f54d-2a53-5373-81a6-355921ea7523" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048315}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120320} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120321} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241026} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241027} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241028} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315094} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317317} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396585} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396694} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396701} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423131}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Vector That Makes a Set Linearly Dependent"} :step/id #uuid "efae78f1-ab53-50ee-aa00-035536697ae6" :step/math-academy-id 41354 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120880}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120880 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121124 :tutorial/content "So far, we've discussed linear dependence and linear independence for sets of two vectors. However, these concepts can be extended to sets of any number of vectors.\n\nA set of $n$ vectors $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_n \\right\\}$ is called **linearly independent** if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 + \\ldots + x_n \\mathbf{v}_n = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis $x_1=x_2=\\ldots=x_n=0.$ Otherwise, if there exists any solution other than $x_1 = x_2 = \\ldots = x_n = 0,$ then we say that the set $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_n \\right\\}$ is **linearly dependent**.\n\nSimilar to sets containing two vectors, a general set of $n$ vectors is dependent if *any* two vectors in the set are parallel.\n\n**But be careful!** When working with sets containing more than two vectors, having a linearly dependent set is possible even if *none* of the vectors are parallel! \n\nFor example, consider the following set of vectors: \n\n![](images/bd/bd4df860628c44295dfd621e6b0e64839c2657c2baa094def656ea8b90ece9c8.svg)\n\nNone of the vectors in this set are parallel. However, we have\n\n![](images/50/5048e293fd6bb60a0ca95451aebd3bb77d0f748bb54e9f5dd044f60fb34c28ce.svg)\n\nSo, the equation\n\n![](images/8a/8ab4013ae0d9241268d040220efcd6184e23548e0685802cd6825b59a7bc71b1.svg)\n\nhas a solution $x_1 = 1,$ $x_2 = 1,$ and $x_3 = -1,$ which is different from $x_1 = x_2 = x_3 = 0.$ Therefore, we conclude that the vectors are linearly dependent." :tutorial/id #uuid "0d550a61-4219-5ffc-83a2-155ddd8fa9f4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6813 :tutorial/title "Linear Dependence and Independence for a General Set of Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "46556bbf-0804-5a49-af3c-3a10e60ee378" :step/math-academy-id 41357 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120881}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120881 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121125 :tutorial/content "As with sets containing only two vectors, any set of vectors that contains the zero vector $\\mathbf{0}$ must be linearly dependent. \n\nTo see this, consider the set \n\n$$\n\\left\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_{n-1}, \\mathbf{0} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v}_1 + 0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v}_2 + \\ldots + 0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v}_{n-1} + 1 \\cdot \\mathbf{0} = \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nSo, the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 + \\ldots + x_{n-1} \\mathbf{v}_{n-1} + x_n \\cdot \\mathbf{0} = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nhas the solution $x_1=x_2=\\ldots = x_{n-1}=0$ and $x_n=1,$ which is different from $x_1=x_2=\\ldots = x_n=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent." :tutorial/id #uuid "7270de59-1d83-5820-aa64-a5f4ca262e3e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6814 :tutorial/title "General Sets of Vectors Containing the Zero Vector"} :step/id #uuid "50295cf6-d08a-5be4-b71d-2116227de4f2" :step/math-academy-id 41358 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120882}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120882 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119924 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119498 :question/id #uuid "7b28b42f-0ac4-543e-baeb-51534aaad480" :question/math-academy-id "e-10907"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb5ca50-e9f3-5513-aff1-1ada8c225a42" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050267}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120147} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120148} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120149} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275381} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275382} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275383} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275384} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275385} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275386} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275387} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275388} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363384} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381146}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent Sets by Inspection"} :step/id #uuid "8247f0d4-142e-58a3-b2ed-d612accf2a6c" :step/math-academy-id 41359 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120883}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120883 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119925 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119499 :question/id #uuid "3843dc13-0908-5d4c-adfe-836a928a1410" :question/math-academy-id "e-10908"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52a45814-d1e2-5948-b7bd-69dcaf6cd0eb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049058} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050178}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120300} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120318} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241031} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241033} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241036} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241038} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317320} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319150} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319157} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345112} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345113} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345114}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent and Dependent Sets Using Row Reduction"} :step/id #uuid "4922cdd4-be71-550d-b216-e5a30b232248" :step/math-academy-id 41362}] :activity/title "Linear Dependence and Independence" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120052}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229993} :activity/id #uuid "02989bdb-eb33-5669-a5fd-ba6ae2e8e96d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050537 :topic/id #uuid "51e12769-3848-5c84-bd86-1c35f7ad69b3" :topic/math-academy-id 6707} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229993 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230210 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $D$ is an $n$th-order differential operator, and the functions $y, y_1,$ and $y_2$ are $n$-times differentiable in some domain of interest. \n\nWe say that a differential operator $D$ is **linear** if the following conditions hold:\n\n- $D(a y(x)) = a D(y(x)),$ where $a$ is a constant.\n\n- $D(y_1(x) + y_2(x)) = D(y_1(x)) + D(y_2(x)).$\n\nWe can combine these two conditions into a single condition as follows:\n\n$$\nD(ay_1(x) + by_2(x)) = aD(y_1(x)) + bD(y_2(x))\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nIt's worth taking a moment to compare these conditions to those we used when defining a *linear transformation*\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T: \\mathbf v \\rightarrow \\mathbf w.\n$$\n\n \n\nFor $\\mathbf T$ to be linear, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $\\mathbf{T}(a\\mathbf{v})=a\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v})$ for any vector $\\mathbf{v}$ and any scalar $a.$\n\n- $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}_1+\\mathbf{v}_2)=\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}_1)+\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}_2)$ for any vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2.$\n\nThe conditions are essentially the same in both cases! Only instead of a transformation $\\mathbf T,$ we have a differential operator $D.$ And instead of vectors as our inputs and outputs, we have functions.\n\nNext, we'll apply this definition to a specific differential operator to see if it is linear." :tutorial/id #uuid "7af65e32-6bf5-5cbf-868a-c08401a21ff4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14617 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "84f3814e-98a9-5834-ac22-59909528ef84" :step/math-academy-id 591452 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229994}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229994 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230211 :tutorial/content "A differential operator $D$ is **linear** if the following linearity condition is satisfied.\n\n$$\nD(ay_1(x) + by_2(x)) = aD(y_1(x)) + bD(y_2(x))\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nLet's consider some examples:\n\n- First, let's consider the following differential operator:\n\n$$\nD_1(y) = x\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} +x^2y\n$$\n\nThis operator is indeed linear. To see this, we need to check that the linearity condition above holds. So we start by applying our operator to the function $y = a y_1(x) + b y_2(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{1}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})+{x}^{2}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince derivatives distribute over sums, and $a$ and $b$ are constants, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{1}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(a{y}_{1})+x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(b{y}_{2})+{x}^{2}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =ax\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}_{1})+bx\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}_{2})+a{x}^{2}{y}_{1}+b{x}^{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =a{{(x\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+{x}^{2}{y}_{1})}_{⏟}}_{{D}_{1}({y}_{1})}+b{{(x\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}+{x}^{2}{y}_{2})}_{⏟}}_{{D}_{1}({y}_{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, since $D_1(ay_1 + by_2) = aD_1(y_1) + bD_1(y_2),$ the operator $D_1$ is linear.\n\n- Now, let's consider the following differential operator:\n\n$$\nD_2(y) = y\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\n$$\n\nThis operator is *not* linear. To see this, we need to check that the linearity condition above does not hold. So we start by applying our operator to the function $y = a y_1(x) + b y_2(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{2}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})(a\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & ={a}^{2}{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+ab{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}+ab{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+{b}^{2}{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to $aD_2(y_1) + bD_2(y_2),$ we see that they are *not* the same:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a{D}_{2}({y}_{1})+b{D}_{2}({y}_{2}) & =a{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}\\ne {D}_{2}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the operator $D_2$ is *not* linear." :tutorial/id #uuid "423cbd55-072d-53de-9269-6dc635abd107" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14620 :tutorial/title "The Linearity Condition"} :step/id #uuid "217461ee-fef0-5c90-a0c9-17bb7b4eec7e" :step/math-academy-id 591453 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229995}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229995 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229137 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228952 :question/id #uuid "d01adef6-d3fb-5cd6-b778-5d44fb6702dd" :question/math-academy-id "e-6572"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "17c792c0-3aec-5e40-8897-2eab9ff2ece0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048459}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229709} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265878} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311877} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311880} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311915} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400457} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400464} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404044}] :knowledge-point/title "Using the Properties of Linear Differential Operators to Identify Them"} :step/id #uuid "60f2a01e-93e7-50c5-9aeb-84890396ee17" :step/math-academy-id 591454 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229996}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229996 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230212 :tutorial/content "An **$n$th-order linear differential operator**, denoted $L$, has the general form:\n\n$$\nL(y) = f_{n}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{n}y}{\\textrm d x^{n}} + f_{n-1}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{n-1}y}{\\textrm d x^{n-1}} + \\cdots + f_{1}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} + f_0(x)y.\n$$\n\nThe functions $f_i(x)$ are called the **coefficient functions**. They must depend only on the variable $x$ and be continuous on the domain of interest. The **order** of the operator is the order of the highest derivative whose coefficient is not zero.\n\nIf all the coefficient functions $f_i(x)$ are constants, we call the operator a **linear operator with constant coefficients**.\n\nLet's look at two examples:\n\n- The operator $L(y) = x^2 y'' - (\\sin x) y' + 2y$ is a 2nd-order *linear differential operator*. Its coefficient functions are $f_2(x) = x^2$, $f_1(x) = -\\sin x$, and $f_0(x) = 2$.\n\n- The operator $L(y) = 4y''' + 7y'' - y$ is a 3rd-order *linear operator with constant coefficients*. Its coefficient functions are $f_3(x) = 4$, $f_2(x) = 7$, $f_1(x) = 0$, and $f_0(x) = -1$." :tutorial/id #uuid "ea3a57ac-0a91-5f3d-85bc-303fef4eb21f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14623 :tutorial/title "The General Form of a Linear Differential Operator"} :step/id #uuid "93ca49af-1d89-5a5d-8223-c645852994cc" :step/math-academy-id 591457 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229997}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229997 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229138 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228933 :question/id #uuid "3c82270a-33f2-53ee-8876-9d8c7cf8291b" :question/math-academy-id "e-21786"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5976004-053f-58ec-9d75-54d43a419397" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229707} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229708} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311881} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311882} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311883} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311884} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311885} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311917}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linear Differential Operators Using the General Form"} :step/id #uuid "8c4cc306-2777-59aa-b11f-63020aec529d" :step/math-academy-id 591458 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229998}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229998 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229139 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228951 :question/id #uuid "04f55d6c-825e-587c-8ef4-a5527d1f374e" :question/math-academy-id "e-6569"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "68148c93-9139-5deb-8a53-a05d63ec5930" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050902}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229748} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229750} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265813} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265814} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265815} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265816} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265817} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335760} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335779} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348246}] :knowledge-point/title "Using Linearity Properties of Linear Differential Operators"} :step/id #uuid "11bd78b5-5bc9-5c73-a208-5e08533f0ffd" :step/math-academy-id 591461}] :activity/title "Linear Differential Operators" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186229264}]}
